@Eomm I know, and it's a bug.. Trust me on this one
The issue is that in your current configuration if an error is thrown from within the handler then it will not be catched, and this is exactly what is happening, I debugged it already. 

when using `p.then(onFulfilled[, onRejected]);` `onRejected` will be triggered if an error occurs on `p`, but if an error occurs within `onFulfilled` it will propagate to the next promise, thus you'll have to catch it down the chain. for that reason, in your specific case, it's better to use this pattern `p.then(onFulfilled).catch(onRejected);` as it will also catch errors coming from `p.then(onFulfilled)` and not just `p`,

With this minimal code I can replicate it:

```js
const fastify = require('./fastify')({ logger: true })

fastify.addHook('preSerialization', async (request, reply, payload) => payload)

fastify.setErrorHandler((error, request, reply) => {
  console.log('setErrorHandler', error)
  reply.send({ custom: error })
})

fastify.get('/:id', {
  schema: {
    response: {
      200: {
        type: 'object',
        required: ['id'],
        properties: { id: { type: 'string' } }
      }
    }
  }
},
function handler () { return { missing: 'id' } }
)

fastify.inject('/asd').then(res => {
  console.log(res.payload)
  console.log(res.statusCode)
})
```

